  <notes>
    <page title="ball" snapshot="1">
      <ViewModel distance="96.43598937988281" far="192.87200927734375" near="0.2410900890827179" parallel="false" stereoAngle="0.0" width="43.39621353149414">
        <LookAtPoint x="0" y="0" z="0"/>
        <UpDirection x="0" y="1" z="0"/>
        <LookDirection x="0" y="0" z="-1"/>
      </ViewModel>
      <content xml:space="preserve">
The Zome ball has 62 holes in icosahedral symmetry.  Thirty rectangles are for blue struts, twenty triangles are for yellow struts, and twelve pentagons are for red struts.      </content>
    </page>
    <page title="blue" snapshot="2">
      <ViewModel distance="96.43598937988281" far="192.87200927734375" near="0.2410900890827179" parallel="false" stereoAngle="0.0" width="43.39621353149414">
        <LookAtPoint x="0" y="0" z="0"/>
        <UpDirection x="0" y="1" z="0"/>
        <LookDirection x="0" y="0" z="-1"/>
      </ViewModel>
      <content xml:space="preserve">
The blue lines define fifteen mirrors, which form a spherical kaleidoscope that gives us the icosahedral reflection group.  Each mirror is orthogonal to a single blue line, and corresponds to two opposite rectangles on the face of the ball.      </content>
    </page>
    <page title="red" snapshot="3">
      <ViewModel distance="96.43598937988281" far="192.87200927734375" near="0.2410900890827179" parallel="false" stereoAngle="0.0" width="43.39621353149414">
        <LookAtPoint x="0" y="0" z="0"/>
        <UpDirection x="0" y="0.525731086730957" z="-0.8506507873535156"/>
        <LookDirection x="0" y="-0.8506507873535156" z="-0.525731086730957"/>
      </ViewModel>
      <content xml:space="preserve">
The red lines form at the intersection of five mirror planes, and therefore give us fivefold rotational symmetry.  This is why red struts have a pentagonal cross-section.      </content>
    </page>
    <page title="yellow" snapshot="4">
      <ViewModel distance="96.43598937988281" far="192.87200927734375" near="0.2410900890827179" parallel="false" stereoAngle="0.0" width="43.39621353149414">
        <LookAtPoint x="0" y="0" z="0"/>
        <UpDirection x="0" y="0.9341723918914795" z="-0.35682210326194763"/>
        <LookDirection x="0" y="-0.35682210326194763" z="-0.9341723322868347"/>
      </ViewModel>
      <content xml:space="preserve">
The yellow lines form at the intersection of three mirror planes, and therefore give us threefold rotational symmetry.  This is why yellow struts have a triangular cross-section.      </content>
    </page>
    <page title=" green" snapshot="18">
      <ViewModel distance="50.36271182243991" far="100.72542364487983" near="0.046632140576333254" parallel="false" stereoAngle="0.0" width="22.66322032009796">
        <LookAtPoint x="5.094155073937996" y="3.301701405432966" z="-0.6948428238960364"/>
        <UpDirection x="-0.007369399838720834" y="0.9988927750533058" z="0.04646413554908355"/>
        <LookDirection x="0.1271427755993227" y="0.04702426270543556" z="-0.9907691120184917"/>
      </ViewModel>
      <content xml:space="preserve">
 Green lines were added to support making diagonals of blue squares, required for several Archimedean solids.      </content>
    </page>
    <page title=" green tip" snapshot="18">
      <ViewModel distance="9.174492073348514" far="18.348984146697028" near="0.008494900067915291" parallel="false" stereoAngle="0.0" width="4.128521433006831">
        <LookAtPoint x="0" y="0" z="0"/>
        <UpDirection x="-0.5516708554407955" y="0.8340533146219745" z="-0.003786241582159973"/>
        <LookDirection x="-0.8199646700245448" y="-0.5431720704035576" z="-0.18061572978301782"/>
      </ViewModel>
      <content xml:space="preserve">
 Green lines go into the pentagon (red strut) holes.      </content>
    </page>
    <page title="  five greens one hole" snapshot="20">
      <ViewModel distance="48.900683009510814" far="97.80136601902163" near="0.045278410193991495" parallel="false" stereoAngle="0.0" width="22.005307354279868">
        <LookAtPoint x="6.901730902241299" y="3.592402176056583" z="-0.574895268878168"/>
        <UpDirection x="0.044112978378568556" y="0.9966025548814523" z="-0.06955136765248175"/>
        <LookDirection x="0.899129156548032" y="-0.06995110363928138" z="-0.43205740700151285"/>
      </ViewModel>
      <content xml:space="preserve">
  There are five ways to put a green strut into a red hole!  We often use blue lines to guide us when adding green struts.      </content>
    </page>
    <page title="frame" snapshot="5">
      <ViewModel distance="74.35712432861328" far="148.71424865722656" near="0.06884919106960297" parallel="false" stereoAngle="0.0" width="33.4607048034668">
        <LookAtPoint x="0" y="0" z="0"/>
        <UpDirection x="-0.22754228115081787" y="0.9730805158615112" z="0.036589786410331726"/>
        <LookDirection x="-0.9450837969779968" y="-0.2297365814447403" z="0.2324601411819458"/>
      </ViewModel>
      <content xml:space="preserve">
Every blue line forms an orthogonal frame with two other blue lines.  There are five frames in total.  This means you can build a compound of five cubes, even though there is no true cubic symmetry in the system.      </content>
    </page>
    <page title="dodecahedron" snapshot="6">
      <ViewModel distance="110.92778778076172" far="221.85560607910156" near="0.2773195505142212" parallel="false" stereoAngle="0.0" width="49.91750717163086">
        <LookAtPoint x="0" y="0" z="0"/>
        <UpDirection x="-0.003705899929627776" y="0.9953262805938721" z="-0.09648610651493073"/>
        <LookDirection x="-0.26995253562927246" y="-0.09389983862638474" z="-0.9582847356796265"/>
      </ViewModel>
      <content xml:space="preserve">
Zome inherently supports building a pentagon, and a dodecahedron, using blue lines.

The center of a dodecahedron is what defines the length of yellow struts relative to blue struts.

Every ball in a connected Zome structure has the same orientation.  Valid structures do not require any bending or twisting.      </content>
    </page>
    <page title="dualize" snapshot="7">
      <ViewModel distance="110.92778778076172" far="221.85560607910156" near="0.2773195505142212" parallel="false" stereoAngle="0.0" width="49.91750717163086">
        <LookAtPoint x="0" y="0" z="0"/>
        <UpDirection x="0.33538469672203064" y="0.12245435267686844" z="-0.9340875148773193"/>
        <LookDirection x="0.031356390565633774" y="-0.9924182891845703" z="-0.1188424602150917"/>
      </ViewModel>
      <content xml:space="preserve">
We can replace every dodecahedron edge with an orthogonal blue strut at one scale larger.      </content>
    </page>
    <page title="duals" snapshot="8">
      <ViewModel distance="110.92778778076172" far="221.85560607910156" near="0.2773195505142212" parallel="false" stereoAngle="0.0" width="49.91750717163086">
        <LookAtPoint x="0" y="0" z="0"/>
        <UpDirection x="0.33538469672203064" y="0.12245435267686844" z="-0.9340875148773193"/>
        <LookDirection x="0.031356390565633774" y="-0.9924182891845703" z="-0.1188424602150917"/>
      </ViewModel>
      <content xml:space="preserve">
Doing this everywhere gives us an icosahedron, the dual of the dodecahedron.      </content>
    </page>
    <page title="icosahedron" snapshot="10">
      <ViewModel distance="108.73126983642578" far="217.4625701904297" near="0.2718282639980316" parallel="false" stereoAngle="0.0" width="48.929073333740234">
        <LookAtPoint x="0" y="0" z="0"/>
        <UpDirection x="0.325926274061203" y="-0.08349509537220001" z="-0.9416991472244263"/>
        <LookDirection x="-0.2653314173221588" y="-0.9641364812850952" z="-0.0063475980423390865"/>
      </ViewModel>
      <content xml:space="preserve">
The center of an icosahedron is what defines the length of red struts relative to blue struts.      </content>
    </page>
    <page title="blue lengths" snapshot="11">
      <ViewModel distance="59.24967575073242" far="118.49935150146484" near="0.054860811680555344" parallel="false" stereoAngle="0.0" width="26.662353515625">
        <LookAtPoint x="3.0907528400421143" y="1.6881613731384277" z="-0.060083016753196716"/>
        <UpDirection x="-0.9995846748352051" y="-0.0007420123438350856" z="0.028808359056711197"/>
        <LookDirection x="0.028809277340769768" y="-0.05020040646195412" z="0.9983235597610474"/>
      </ViewModel>
      <content xml:space="preserve">
Zometool struts come in three sizes, with scaling between them by the golden ratio.      </content>
    </page>
    <page title="long blues" snapshot="12">
      <ViewModel distance="71.08212280273438" far="142.16424560546875" near="0.06581678241491318" parallel="false" stereoAngle="0.0" width="31.986957550048828">
